# Everything here is sent to the Docker daemon as build context before the
# first instruction runs, so anything left out of this file slows down every
# build whether or not the Dockerfile copies it.
#
# The worst offenders are node_modules (hundreds of MB, and reinstalled inside
# the image anyway from package-lock.json) and dist-offline/, which contains a
# ~330 MB tar of the very images being built.

node_modules
**/node_modules
dist-offline

# Build output. Both images compile from source inside the container, so host
# artifacts would at best be redundant and at worst be stale ones baked in.
dist
**/dist
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Local runtime state: stored SBOM blobs, screenshots from the UI drive.
var
**/var

# Secrets. The compose file injects configuration as environment variables;
# a .env copied into an image layer would be readable by anyone who pulls it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.docker.example
deploy/.env
deploy/CREDENTIALS.txt

.git
.gitignore
.github

# Docs and scripts that play no part in a build.
README.md
deploy
ci-templates
scripts
**/*.md
!packages/*/README.md

# Test material. Images are built from source that has already been tested on
# the build machine; running tests inside the image build would need the dev
# dependencies the runtime stage deliberately drops.
**/test
**/*.test.ts

.vscode
.idea
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
